  "concerns": [
    {
      "id": "thm-lifting-of-indecomposable-projectives-to-the-deformed-family",
      "concern": "Proof 1.1 says, without a middle-term qualification, 'Kernels, cokernels and extensions remain in this category'. Arbitrary U(g)⊗R-module extensions do not preserve the required relative-weight decomposition. For sl₂, induce the free rank-two Borel module with ev=ew=0, hv=(ξ+τ(h))v and hw=(ξ+τ(h))w+v. PBW gives an extension of Δ_R(ξ) by itself, but its highest space has a nonzero Jordan part for h−τ(h), so its middle term is not a relative-weight module. The opened published theorem thm-category-o-is-abelian-and-extension-closed explicitly imposes the middle-term weight hypothesis and proves the corresponding ordinary counterexample in Step 5.1. The lifting proof must qualify its extension claim as occurring among relative-weight modules. This finding concerns that overstrong ancillary assertion; it does not itself refute the projective-lifting conclusion.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-lifting-of-indecomposable-projectives-to-the-deformed-family",
      "concern": "The singular part of the Statement and Proof 7.1 uses 'distinct cosets W/W_ν' without defining W_ν there. The inherited def-integral-weyl-group-of-a-weight uses W_ν for the integral-reflection group, which equals W for integral ν; the lifting argument instead requires Stab_W(ν+ρ). The later central-action lemma explicitly makes this distinction, but this supplier should fix its own stabilizer notation.",
      "severity": "presentation"
    },
    {
      "id": "def-the-antidominant-projective-and-the-soergel-functor",
      "concern": "The final paragraph asserts: 'Every simple submodule of a Verma is itself a simple Verma: its universal Verma map into that Verma is injective.' This silently uses lem-a-nonzero-verma-homomorphism-is-injective, which is neither linked nor listed in this item's deps. Its opened two-step PBW/domain proof supplies the missing inference immediately. Declare that supplier or supply the elementary argument, since the resulting unique-socle assertion is used by the Struktursatz.",
      "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
    }
  ],
